A||N||6||Chance||Hard/Non-Shifting Clutch Problem Solved!||||10||
Z||000000||Chance||03-03-2001||05:16 AM||||    -=&lt;1986 GT w/5 speed Getrag&gt;=-<BR>     Well, after several days of calling local area mech's and transmission specialist, I decided to take it to this one place that more or less told me "Come on down, check out our shop, I'll look at it etc., because I don't like to give phone estimates.  Well, I took it down there, and the owner said the mechanics leave at 5:00 but he stays till 5:30 (I got there about 4:55) He took care of a few other people real quick, and about 5:20, I pulled it in.  He got some tools, and we opened the back, and he watched me push the clutch, to see how much "push" was on the levers (we also seen the slave rod wasn't in the lever "cup" we spent 15-20 minutes taking the intake hose off, and loosening the slave to slip the rod back in place.  I tried pushing the pedal again, no/little movement.  I told him, I suspected the t/o bearing either pushed through the clutch fingers, or the clutch fork... he thought the slave, or master was bad.  He got his power bleeder, and we bled the slave, still no lever movement.  (also note that when all my problems started, a few weeks after it quit shifting/no clutch engagement, I noticed a noise when driving, then a week later no more bad noise) We stayed there till close to 7:00 looking at it, and I told him that I would bring it in tomorrow to drop it off so they could test it more.  He opened the door, I put it in reverse, and started it... the bad noise from inside the traney was there again (ah ha, the rod/lever was pushing on something to make it do that) I backed up, and then he said "Yeah, I do hear that, your right, it is inside it, You want to leave it here, and I can take you home, that sounds pretty bad.  I told him thats O.k. I would limp it home, and then  just head back tomorrow, so I did.  That was about 2-3 weeks ago on a monday/tuesday.  He called me friday the next week to appoligise about taking so long, but he said when he pulled it out, that the fork was either bent, or busted, and basicly, just a mess in there and was surprised it didn't totally trash it.  He was waiting on a wire clip/spring  from GM that I guess keeps the t/o bearing and fork togeter, or from slipping off each other. When he tore the transmission apart, he couldn't find it in there, and said that an update sheet included with the clutch kit or service update said "clip MUST be installed for GM to honor warrenty" (maybe the paper was included in the new clutch fork) ANYWAY, he talked to me about an hour and a half, told me he's saving all the parts for me to look at, and that he was doing the work himself, and was sorry it seemed like it was taking so long.  He said he while they had it out, they totally cleaned up the transmission and the shifter area. (It was a mess from all the white lithium greese/wd40/etc trying to lubricate the cables and shifter) I told him when I dropped it off, that if it was getting close to the 600 dollar mark to fix it, then call me so we can see what needs to be done, but when I asked if it was close to 600 bucks yet, he said "No, should get that high, just waiting for that spring clip.  Resurfaced your flywheel, and had a new clutch fork here in our stock (100 buck for the fork he told me) and that he wanted to tell me he's never had a problem with any of the Napa clutchs he's installed and they have a 12,000/1year (another had a 15,000/1year) warrenty."  Yeah, so far, its been a piece of cake getting it out, could of had it to you by the weekend if all the parts would of been in, but so far, it hasn't been that difficult to do, and I have all the service manuals on CD rom, so that helps... I will call you when its done, or you call/come it to check up on it.<BR>     So far, I have been impressed with this shop! the owner has taken the time to talk to me, and look at the problem, and explain everything that was going on.  While on the phone, he said he picked up a HotRod magazine that had 4 fieros on the cover, and was amazed that they can cram some big engines in them, and thought that it would be cool to have one with a Northstar in it! He said he believes in doing it right the first time, and if you know anyone who needs their Fieros worked on, clutch or whatever, go ahead and send em here if you like my work, I really don't know why you had such a hard time finding someone willing to replace a clutch, its not that hard at all! and told me that he appriacates referels and if he does good work, then it will just help him get more work! (and he gives out referal bucks if they mention your name) So far, so good!  I hope his work is as good as his customer service has been so far.  Makes me realize that I was totally screwed out of 1200 bucks when I had the other place I took it do it close to 2 years ago.  (I figure "Sharps" either 1. Didn't have any Fiero reference/shop manuals 2. Didn't think that spring/clip was needed and the clutch would last longer then their 6month warrenty and no one would be the wiser!)<BR>&lt;btw Kong, was your wire clip still in there?&gt;<BR>Later Chance<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000001||Chance||03-03-2001||05:22 AM||||-=&lt;86 GT 5 sp Getrag&gt;=-<BR>I also told him to use new parts, no rebuilt ones.<P>Chance<BR>   ?||||reg||4
Z||000002||Formula88||03-03-2001||05:14 PM||||Great to hear you found a good mechanic.  He's right.  The clutch isn't bad of a job, if you have a shop, lift, etc.  I'm doing mine at home right now, and while it's a big job, it's not so much a PITA.  Just a lot to do.  My job could have been done in 1 day, if I had all the parts necessary and the full support of a shop.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Chance||03-03-2001||05:45 PM||||I HOPE it turns out good.  If I had a lift/shop/full compliment of tools... I would of gladly done it myself.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Bruce||03-03-2001||07:41 PM||||Chance,<BR>Sounds just like my situation: $1200 in clutch repair about 2 yrs ago for a pretty crummy job. If I'ld known better then, I would have had one of Fiero Shop's clutches installed, rather than NAPA. When they say 12,000 miles, that's what you can expect.<BR>Glad you found an honest mechanic, though.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Chance||03-03-2001||11:33 PM||||Was it a new clutch kit, or rebuild from NAPA? I looked at the different ones at <A HREF="http://www.napaonline.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.napaonline.com</A>  but after having clutch/shifting problems for close to a year and looking at all the different clutches etc. I just get more confused.  I just want it done right. (His shop is a NAPA service center btw, thats why the NAPA parts)<p>[This message has been edited by Chance (edited 03-03-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000006||Wade87GT||03-04-2001||07:55 PM||||Formula88, are you pulling the engine/trans/cradle assembly or just the trans? Is there any way to do the job without pulling the axles? My clutch just went out again, sounds like somethings broken in there. The last replacement was 6 years/75,000 miles ago. Aamco replaced it for $1100. and they did an excellent job. But...||||reg||9
